⍝ AllPrimitives.tc
⍝ ════════════════

⊣««« ⍝ Function ON_ERROR ARGS: write /tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_ERROR_N.apl:
 a self-contained script that reconstructs A, B, X (via 10 ⎕CR)
 and then executes OPER, so the failure captured in
 ARGS←(N A B X OPER) can be reproduced later.
»»»

∇ON_ERROR ARGS;N;A;B;X;OPER;FH;LINES;NAME;FMT;TSTR
 (N A B X OPER)←ARGS
 NAME←'/tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_ERROR_%d.apl' ⎕FIO.sprintf N
 FH←'w' ⎕FIO.fopen NAME
 FMT←'%s',(⎕UCS 10)
 TSTR←'⍝ %d-%d-%d %d:%d:%d' ⎕FIO.sprintf 6↑⎕TS
 ⊣((⊂FMT),⊂TSTR) ⎕FIO.fprintf FH
 LINES←(10⎕CR 'A'),(10⎕CR 'B'),(10⎕CR 'X'),⊂OPER
 ⊣{⊣((⊂FMT),⊂⍵) ⎕FIO.fprintf FH}¨LINES
 ⊣⎕FIO.fclose FH
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function Z←ALL_PRIMITIVES: run for CONTEXT.SECONDS seconds,
 repeatedly calling ONE_PRIMITIVE and accounting for successes
 vs. violations/failures.
»»»

∇Z←ALL_PRIMITIVES; ⎕IO;T0;LCNT;ECNT;PREV_BUDGET;PREV_TD;R
 ⊣««« ⍝ 0 ⎕RVAL B requires B to be a rank-1 byte vector (length 0, 8, 16,
  ..., 256), hence the (8⍴256)⊤ encoding -- plain 0 12 31 24 60 60
  1000⊥⎕TS alone would collapse to a scalar and fail with RANK ERROR.
 »»»

 ⊣0 ⎕RVAL (8⍴256)⊤0 12 31 24 60 60 1000⊥⎕TS   ⍝ seed from current time
 ⊣««« ⍝ 5⎕RVAL / 3⎕RVAL change ⎕RVAL's global generator state, which
  outlives this function -- remember the previous settings so they
  can be restored before returning, or every ⎕RVAL call made by
  unrelated code for the rest of the session would silently inherit
  this fuzzer's budget/type mix (that leak once broke Domino_stress.tc
  and Macros.tc, which assume ⎕RVAL's default settings).
 »»»

 PREV_BUDGET←5 ⎕RVAL CONTEXT.BUDGET   ⍝ apply the ⍴∈ limit
 PREV_TD←3 ⎕RVAL CONTEXT.TD           ⍝ apply the type distribution
 CONTEXT.PRIM←6 ⎕RVAL ⍬
 T0←24 60 60⊥⎕TS[4 5 6]
 LCNT←0              ⍝ number of loop iterations so far
 ECNT←0              ⍝ number of ON_ERROR dumps so far
LOOP:
 ⍝ checked once per iteration here (not just on the violation path
 ⍝ below), so the time budget is enforced regardless of outcome.

 →((24 60 60⊥⎕TS[4 5 6]) ≥ T0 + CONTEXT.SECONDS)/TIMEOUT
 LCNT←LCNT+1
 ⊣⎕DL 0.1   ⍝ rate limit (unconditional: caps load on server72 every iteration)
 ⍝ R.OK, and (if R.OK is 0) R.A R.B R.X R.OPER

 R←ONE_PRIMITIVE
 →(R.OK)/LOOP                 ⍝ invariant holds: keep fuzzing
 ⊣««« ⍝ ON_ERROR needs the incremented count before the branch decides
  whether to keep going, so it takes its own (ECNT+1) rather than
  sharing the assignment folded into the branch below.
 »»»

 ⍝ dump a reproducer for each of the first 10 errors

 ON_ERROR (ECNT+1) R.A R.B R.X R.OPER
 →((ECNT←ECNT+1)<10)/LOOP       ⍝ keep fuzzing until the 10th error
 Z←0 ◊ →RESTORE
TIMEOUT:
 Z←1
RESTORE:
 ⍝ undo the global ⎕RVAL state change from CONTEXT.BUDGET/CONTEXT.TD
 ⍝ above, so the rest of the session sees ⎕RVAL's usual defaults again.

 ⊣5 ⎕RVAL PREV_BUDGET
 ⊣3 ⎕RVAL PREV_TD
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function ZZ←ONE_PRIMITIVE: run one fuzzing iteration: pick a
 random primitive row from CONTEXT.PRIM (the ⎕RVAL fuzz table),
 generate A/B/X for it, execute it, and check its invariant.

 Returns a structured ZZ (a GNU APL extension): ZZ.OK is 1 if the
 invariant held, 0 if it was violated or if generation/execution
 itself failed; ZZ.A ZZ.B ZZ.X ZZ.OPER are the exact values used,
 for ON_ERROR's reproducer dump when ZZ.OK is 0.
»»»

∇ZZ←ONE_PRIMITIVE;ROW;OPER;EXPD;RC;ET;RES;RC2;ET2;RES2;RCG;ETG;RESG;A;B;X;Z
 ⊣««« ⍝ Z is deliberately declared local here even though it's never read:
  every row's OPER string is literally "Z←...", e.g. "Z←A+B" (baked
  into the ⎕RVAL fuzz table). Without a local Z, that assignment
  would reach the caller's own Z through dynamic scoping instead --
  and since the result variable here is ZZ rather than Z, the
  caller in question is ALL_PRIMITIVES, whose own result IS named Z.
 »»»

 ⊣««« ⍝ B is generated first; A_stim="~B" (set for rval.def rows whose Z
  constraint requires (⍴A)⌈⍴B, i.e. scalar-extension pairs like A+B)
  then calls 7 ⎕RVAL B to conform A to B (scalar or exactly ⍴B) instead
  of generating A independently and hoping it happens to fit.
  TODO: many other primitives (replicate A/B, reshape A⍴B, encode/decode
  A⊤B/A⊥B, catenate, ...) still need their own per-primitive conforming
  rule in ⎕RVAL; for now they keep generating A independently.
 »»»

 ⍝ pick a random primitive: arity A_stim B_stim X_stim Z_constraint

 ROW←(CONTEXT.PRIM)[?≢CONTEXT.PRIM;]
 ⊣««« ⍝ rval.def's ecount column is the literal placeholder token "RE"
  (see rval.def's own header comment: "the APL test runner substitutes
  a concrete value... before calling ⎕RVAL"); replace it here with
  CONTEXT.BUDGET before ⍎'ing A_stim/B_stim/X_stim below, or every
  single generation call fails with VALUE ERROR (RE is not an APL
  name) -- which is exactly what happened before this fix: 100% of
  fuzz iterations silently counted as "violations".
 »»»

 ROW[2 3 4]←{c←('RE'≡¯2↑⍵)∧2≤≢⍵ ⋄ (1+c)⊃⍵((¯2↓⍵),⍕CONTEXT.BUDGET)}¨ROW[2 3 4]
 ⍝ arity expression (e.g. Z←A+B) and Z constraint, set first so
 ⍝ the caller always has a valid OPER even if generation fails below

 (OPER EXPD)←ROW[1 5]
 ⊣««« ⍝ operator-taking primitives (f/,f¨,f.g,...): substitute a real
  function for the placeholder(s) (?N already respects ⎕IO —
  no +1 needed)
 »»»

 OPER[⍸OPER='f']←(CONTEXT.FPOOL)[?(+/OPER='f')⍴5]
 OPER[⍸OPER='g']←(CONTEXT.FPOOL)[?(+/OPER='g')⍴5]
 A←⍬ ◊ B←⍬ ◊ X←⍬             ⍝ safe defaults in case generation fails below
 ⊣««« ⍝ B is generated first, so that A (and X) can conform to B's ACTUAL
  shape/rank instead of being generated independently and hoping they
  happen to fit (per SVN r2037 discussion: fix this inside ⎕RVAL, not
  by post-hoc patching in this function).
 »»»

 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '{⎕RVAL⍣(0<≢⍵)⊢⍵}⍎3⊃ROW'
 →(RCG≠1)/FAIL          ⍝ generation itself failed (e.g. a bad rval.def stim)
 B←RESG
 →(~(2⊃ROW)≡'~B')/NOTPLAINB
 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '7 ⎕RVAL B'   ⍝ A_stim="~B": conform to B (scalar or ⍴B)
 →GOTA
NOTPLAINB:
 ⍝ any other ~xxx marker (~BL, ~BF, ~S, ...; see CONFORM_A) also
 ⍝ conforms A to B, just with a different rule than plain ~B. Checked
 ⍝ by leading '~' rather than requiring the CONFORM_A-marker name to
 ⍝ start with "~B" specifically -- ~S (always scalar) doesn't.
 →(~(0<≢2⊃ROW)∧((,'~')≡1↑2⊃ROW))/NORMALA
 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '(2⊃ROW) CONFORM_A B'
 →GOTA
NORMALA:
 ⍝ still independent for other primitives

 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '{⎕RVAL⍣(0<≢⍵)⊢⍵}⍎2⊃ROW'
GOTA:
 →(RCG≠1)/FAIL
 A←RESG
 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '{⎕RVAL⍣(0<≢⍵)⊢⍵}⍎4⊃ROW'
 →(RCG≠1)/FAIL
 X←RESG
 ⊣««« ⍝ X_stim is still generated independently of B, with no idea what B's
  actual rank turned out to be — that produced axis values with no
  relation to B at all (observed: values like 7509335927660159858,
  causing WS FULL in e.g. ⊃[X]B). Every axis-taking primitive in
  rval.def is [X]B, so derive a valid axis from B's real rank instead,
  when one is wanted (X_stim non-empty) and B actually has axes.
 »»»

 →((0=≢X)∨0=≢⍴B)/NOAXIS
 X←?≢⍴B
NOAXIS:
 ⍝ execute OPER; on success (RC=2) RES is the value OPER returned

 (RC ET RES)←⎕EC OPER
 →(RC≠2)/FAIL           ⍝ OPER itself failed (e.g. non-conforming random A/B)
 ⍝ evaluate the invariant; a plain expr gives RC=1 on success

 (RC2 ET2 RES2)←⎕EC EXPD
 →(RC2≠1)/FAIL          ⍝ the constraint expression itself failed to evaluate
 ⍝ invariant held (1) or was violated (0)

 ZZ.OK←RES2
 ZZ.A←A ◊ ZZ.B←B ◊ ZZ.X←X ◊ ZZ.OPER←OPER
 →0
FAIL:
 ZZ.OK←0
 ZZ.A←A ◊ ZZ.B←B ◊ ZZ.X←X ◊ ZZ.OPER←OPER
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function Z←MARKER CONFORM_A B: generate a value A that structurally
 conforms to B per the rule named by MARKER, one of:
   ~BL  same rank as B, all axes but the Last one matching B, or scalar
   ~BF  same rank as B, all axes but the First one matching B, or scalar
   ~BC  rank-1 non-negative-int vector, length = B's last axis (for
        A/B, A⊂B: the Count/boundary vector must match B's last axis
        exactly, or LENGTH ERROR). Approximation for the [X] variants
        too: X is derived from B's rank only *after* A is generated
        (see ONE_PRIMITIVE), so the actual axis isn't known yet --
        conforming to the last axis is right whenever X turns out to
        be the last axis, and wrong (LENGTH ERROR, same as before this
        fix) otherwise.
   ~BE  boolean vector for A\B (expand): unlike A/B, values must be
        strictly 0/1 (DOMAIN ERROR otherwise) and it's the *count* of
        1s, not the total length, that must equal B's last axis; the
        rest are zero-fill positions, so total length is >= that.
   ~BR  rank-1 int vector, length = ⍴⍴B exactly (A↓B: one drop-count
        per axis of B; magnitude is irrelevant -- drop clamps, so any
        int works), or scalar.
   ~BD  same rank as B minus 1, shape = B's leading axes (i.e. ⍴B with
        the last axis dropped entirely, not just varied -- unlike
        ~BL); or scalar. For A⌽B: one rotate-amount per row, magnitude
        irrelevant since rotate wraps around.
   ~BDF same as ~BD but drops B's *first* axis instead (for A⊖B, which
        rotates along the first axis: one amount per column).
   ~S   always scalar (int). For A⌽[X]B: a vector A would need to match
        B's shape minus axis X specifically, but X is derived only
        *after* A is generated (see ONE_PRIMITIVE) -- scalar A always
        works regardless of which axis X turns out to be, so it's used
        unconditionally here rather than only as one of two outcomes.
   ~BP  permutation-like: length ⍴⍴B, each value independently 1..⍴⍴B
        (for A⍉B: repeats are legal -- generalized/diagonal transpose
        -- so this isn't a strict permutation, just right-length and
        in-range). ⍬ if B is scalar.
   ~BI  index vector for A⌷B: length ⍴⍴B, element i independently
        1..(⍴B)[i] (a valid index into B's i-th axis specifically, not
        just any value 1..⍴⍴B like ~BP). ⍬ if B is scalar.
   ~BCF like ~BC but conforms to B's *first* axis instead of last (for
        A⌿B, replicate-along-first-axis).
   ~BEF like ~BE but conforms to B's *first* axis instead of last (for
        A⍀B, expand-along-first-axis).
   ~BDEC decode (A⊥B): A's *last* axis must equal B's *first* axis (or
        A scalar); generates A as a vector of exactly that length
        (under-tests the general case where A has other leading axes
        too, but is always structurally valid).
   ~BMM  matrix-multiply-style (Af.gB, inner product): A and B are
        both rank-2 (per B_stim/A_stim already fixing rank=2); A's
        column count (last axis) must equal B's row count (first
        axis). Generates A with a fresh random row count and column
        count = B's row count.
   ~BDOM matrix divide (A⌹B, DOMINO): B is rank-2 (per B_stim already
        fixing rank=2); A's *first* axis (row count) must equal B's
        first axis, A itself rank 1 (vector) or rank 2 (matrix), with
        any column count when rank 2.
   ~FMT  dyadic format (A⍕B): A must have length 1 or 2 and be
        non-negative (LENGTH/DOMAIN ERROR otherwise); generates a
        1-or-2-element vector of small non-negative ints.
   ~BIDX bracket index (A[B]): B (generated first, the array) must be
        rank 1 (RANK ERROR on a matrix); A (the index) can be any
        shape, values must be valid indices 1..≢B.
   ~BPICK pick (A⊃B): B rank 0 (scalar) needs A=⍬; B rank 1 needs A a
        plain scalar index 1..≢B; B rank≥2 needs A=⊂ of an
        (⍴⍴B)-element vector, element i forced into 1..(⍴B)[i]
        (confirmed directly: a flat, non-enclosed multi-element A into
        a matrix B is RANK ERROR, must be enclosed as one index-path).
   ~BIJ  multi-axis bracket index (B[I;J], B[I;J;K]): B's rank fixes
        the number of semicolon-separated axes (2 or 3, via B_stim).
        rval.def's arity string decomposes A itself into per-axis
        index vectors -- "Z←B[⊃A[1];⊃A[2]]" etc -- rather than
        needing ONE_PRIMITIVE to generate separate I/J/K variables
        (it only ever generates A/B/X). A is generated here as an
        (⍴⍴B)-element NESTED vector; element i is itself a vector of
        random length 0..10 (an empty index vector is legal -- B[⍬;J]
        selects a 0-row slice, confirmed directly), values forced into
        1..(⍴B)[i]. Z_constraint then checks ⍴Z against (≢⊃A[1]),
        (≢⊃A[2]) etc, matching B[I;J]'s real shape rule (confirmed
        directly: ⍴(B[I;J]) ≡ (≢I),(≢J), independent per-axis lengths,
        not requiring ≢I=≢J or any relation to B's own shape).
   ~SC  shape/take-count vector for A⍴B and A↑B: rank-1, independent of
        B, length 0..8 (matching the original raw spec these rows used
        before this marker existed). Generated by asking ⎕RVAL for a
        THROWAWAY array's shape rather than 8 independent magnitude-
        capped ints: ⎕RVAL already clips a generated array's own shape
        internally so ×/shape ≤ CONTEXT.BUDGET (Quad_RVAL.cc's
        do_eval_B), and since ×/R ≥ ⌈/R for non-negative R, that
        product bound also bounds every individual axis length -- no
        separate per-element cap needed, and the guarantee is tied to
        the same budget the rest of the fuzzer already respects rather
        than an arbitrary extra constant.
        Found via the fuzzer: A↑B with 8 independently huge-magnitude
        (near int64_max) int elements in A crashed the interpreter
        outright (SEGV in Bif_F12_TAKE::fill, via a wrapped-negative
        volume that slipped past Value::init_ravel()'s "small value"
        fast path and got a too-small buffer); A⍴B with the same shape
        of input didn't crash but silently produced a corrupted Value
        (⍴ and ≢ report the bogus huge shape, but indexing it throws
        INDEX ERROR -- worse than a crash, since it looks like it
        worked). Both rows used the exact same unbounded raw A_stim
        before this fix. Root cause: Shape::add_shape_item()'s
        "volume *= len" has no overflow check, by design -- it's on a
        hot path, and the maintainer's call was that generators must
        never hand it values whose product can overflow, rather than
        paying for a check there.
 rval.def's A_stim field is preprocessor-tokenized (unquoted), so it
 cannot hold an arbitrary APL expression -- MARKER is a bare ASCII
 string instead, and this function does the real work in real APL.
»»»

∇Z←MARKER CONFORM_A B;RB;OV;LASTAX;N;EXTRA;I
 RB←⍴⍴B
 →(MARKER≡'~BL')/LASTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BF')/FIRSTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BC')/COUNTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BE')/EXPANDVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BR')/RANKVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BD')/DROPVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BDF')/DROPVARYFIRST
 →(MARKER≡'~S')/INTSCALAR
 →(MARKER≡'~BP')/PERMVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BI')/INDEXVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BCF')/COUNTVARYFIRST
 →(MARKER≡'~BEF')/EXPANDVARYFIRST
 →(MARKER≡'~BDEC')/DECODEVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BMM')/MATMULVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BDOM')/DOMINOVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~FMT')/FMTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BIDX')/BIDXVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BPICK')/PICKVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BIJ')/BIJVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~SC')/SHAPECNTVARY
 Z←⍬ ◊ →0                     ⍝ unknown marker (shouldn't happen)
LASTVARY:
 →(0=RB)/SCALARFORM
 OV←RB((¯1↓⍴B),¯1+?101)(1 1 1 1 1)3 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 →DONE
FIRSTVARY:
 →(0=RB)/SCALARFORM
 OV←RB((¯1+?101),1↓⍴B)(1 1 1 1 1)3 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 →DONE
COUNTVARY:
 LASTAX←1⌈¯1↑⍴B                ⍝ B's last axis size, or 1 if B scalar (¯1↑⍬ fills 0)
 OV←1 LASTAX (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←4|⎕RVAL OV                  ⍝ ⎕RVAL ints are unbounded magnitude and
 →0                            ⍝ signed; 4| forces 0..3, non-negative
EXPANDVARY:
 LASTAX←1⌈¯1↑⍴B
 EXTRA←(?11)-1                 ⍝ 0..10 extra zero-fill slots
 N←LASTAX+EXTRA
 Z←(LASTAX⍴1),EXTRA⍴0
 Z←Z[N?N]                      ⍝ shuffle so the 1s land at random spots
 →0
RANKVARY:
 →(0=RB)/SCALARFORM
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
DROPVARY:
 →(RB≤1)/INTSCALAR              ⍝ RB=0: scalar; RB=1: no leading axes left
 OV←(RB-1)((¯1↓⍴B))(0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV                ⍝ cap magnitude: GNU APL's ⌽/⊖ are O(N)
 →0                             ⍝ in the rotate amount, huge values hang
DROPVARYFIRST:
 →(RB≤1)/INTSCALAR              ⍝ RB=0: scalar; RB=1: no trailing axes left
 OV←(RB-1)((1↓⍴B))(0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
INTSCALAR:
 OV←0 ⍬ (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
PERMVARY:
 →(0=RB)/EMPTYFORM
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+(RB⍴RB)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1        ⍝ 1..RB regardless of ⎕RVAL's own sign
 →0
INDEXVARY:
 →(0=RB)/EMPTYFORM
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+(⍴B)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1           ⍝ element i forced into 1..(⍴B)[i]
 →0
EMPTYFORM:
 Z←⍬
 →0
COUNTVARYFIRST:
 LASTAX←1⌈1↑⍴B,1                ⍝ B's first axis size, or 1 if B scalar
 OV←1 LASTAX (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←4|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
EXPANDVARYFIRST:
 LASTAX←1⌈1↑⍴B,1
 EXTRA←(?11)-1
 N←LASTAX+EXTRA
 Z←(LASTAX⍴1),EXTRA⍴0
 Z←Z[N?N]
 →0
DECODEVARY:
 LASTAX←1⌈1↑⍴B,1                ⍝ B's first axis size, or 1 if B scalar
 OV←1 LASTAX (0 1 1 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
MATMULVARY:
 LASTAX←1⌈1↑⍴B,1                ⍝ B's row count (first axis)
 OV←2((¯1+?101),LASTAX)(0 1 1 1 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
DOMINOVARY:
 LASTAX←1⌈1↑⍴B,1                ⍝ B's row count (first axis)
 →((?2)=1)/DOMINOVEC
 OV←2(LASTAX,¯1+?101)(0 1 1 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
DOMINOVEC:
 OV←1(,LASTAX)(0 1 1 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
FMTVARY:
 N←?2                          ⍝ length 1 or 2 (⎕IO=1: ?2 is already 1 or 2)
 OV←1(,N)(0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←21|⎕RVAL OV                 ⍝ non-negative, small (0..20)
 →0
BIDXVARY:
 N←1⌈≢B                        ⍝ ≢B is B's own tally (works for scalar too)
 OV←¯2 ¯5 (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+N|(⎕RVAL OV)-1            ⍝ every element forced into 1..≢B
 →0
PICKVARY:
 →(0=RB)/EMPTYFORM             ⍝ scalar B: A must be ⍬
 →(1=RB)/PICKSCALAR            ⍝ vector B: A is a plain scalar index
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⊂1+(⍴B)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1        ⍝ rank≥2: one enclosed RB-element index
 →0                            ⍝ path, element i forced into 1..(⍴B)[i]
PICKSCALAR:
 OV←0 ⍬ (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+(≢B)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1         ⍝ scalar index forced into 1..≢B
 →0
BIJVARY:
 Z←⍬ ◊ I←0
BIJLOOP:
 I←I+1
 →(I>RB)/0                     ⍝ Z now has RB nested per-axis index vectors
 N←(?11)-1                     ⍝ this axis's index-vector length, 0..10
 OV←1(,N)(0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←Z,⊂1+((⍴B)[I])|(⎕RVAL OV)-1 ⍝ values forced into 1..(⍴B)[I]
 →BIJLOOP
SHAPECNTVARY:
 ⍝ generate a THROWAWAY array and take its OWN shape as Z, instead of
 ⍝ generating up to 8 magnitude-capped ints independently: ⎕RVAL already
 ⍝ clips a generated array's shape internally so ×/(that shape) ≤
 ⍝ CONTEXT.BUDGET (see Quad_RVAL.cc's do_eval_B), and since ×/R ≥ ⌈/R
 ⍝ for non-negative R, that ×/⍴Z≤budget bound also bounds every
 ⍝ individual axis length in Z -- no separate per-element cap needed,
 ⍝ and unlike the discarded 100| cap this ties the guarantee to the
 ⍝ same budget the rest of the fuzzer already respects.
 OV←¯8 ¯1000 (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⍴⎕RVAL OV
 →0
SCALARFORM:
 OV←0 ⍬ (1 1 1 1 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
DONE:
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
∇

      ⊣««« ⍝ CONTEXT is a set of parameters that govern the testcase
       execution in all sub-functions. To be adapted by the user
       as needed. Uses structured variables (a GNU APL extension).
      »»»

      CONTEXT.BUDGET←10000        ⍝ desired ⍴∈ limit, see 5 ⎕RVAL B
      CONTEXT.TD ← 15 70 15 5 5   ⍝ type distribution: char, int,
                                  ⍝ double, complex, nested (in %)
      CONTEXT.SECONDS←5           ⍝ how long ALL_PRIMITIVES fuzzes
      ⊣««« ⍝ CONTEXT.FPOOL: scalar dyadic (also valid monadic) functions
       that substitute for the 'f'/'g' placeholders used by
       operator-taking primitives in the ⎕RVAL fuzz table
       (e.g. f/, f¨, f.g).
      »»»

      CONTEXT.FPOOL←'+-×⌈⌊'

      ALL_PRIMITIVES
1

      )ERASE CONTEXT

      )CHECK BRIEF

